// Package builtin documents the PGT builtin functions, like builtin.go does for Go.
// It is never compiled or loaded: importing it with #use "builtin" is an error.
// The compiler recognizes these names itself; the signatures below are descriptive, not real code.
//
// Console output and input are not builtins: they live in the std/fmt package (#use "fmt"),
// which is written in PGT on top of the any type and the reflection builtins below.
// Calling print, println, input or inputln without fmt is a compile error.
//
// Runtime failures (panic, out-of-range index, nil, division by zero) are implemented
// in PGT in the std/runtime package, which is linked into every program implicitly.

// fn panic(msg string)
// panic prints "panic: <msg>" to stderr, flushes stdout and terminates the program with exit code 2.
// The compiler turns the call into runtime.panicString. The argument is converted to a string.

// fn exit(code int)
// exit flushes stdout and terminates the program immediately with the given code.

// fn len(x) int
// len returns the length of a string, array or slice.

// fn append(a []T, items ...T) []T
// append returns the array with the items added; if capacity is insufficient it allocates a new block.

// fn make([]T, len int, cap int) []T
// make creates an array of zero values with length len and capacity cap (cap is optional). A negative length panics.

// fn new(T) *T
// new allocates a zero-initialized value of type T and returns a pointer to it.

// fn malloc(free[T] n) []T
// malloc allocates n elements of type T manually, for var&; implemented in std/memory (memory.alloc).

// fn free(x []T)
// free releases a block allocated by malloc; calling free again is safe (memory.release).

// fn unsafeData(a []T) *T
// unsafeData returns a pointer to the array's data.

// fn unsafeSlice(p *T, n int64) []T
// unsafeSlice wraps a block of memory as an array of length and capacity n, without reference counting.

// fn str(x) string
// str converts a value to a string.

// fn chars(s string) []char
// chars splits a string into characters.

// fn parseInt(s string) int
// fn parseInt64(s string) int64
// fn parseFloat(s string) float
// These functions parse a number from a string.

// fn substr(s string, start int, n int) string
// substr returns a substring.

// fn indexOf(s string, sub string) int
// indexOf returns the index of the first occurrence of sub in s, or -1.

// fn upper(s string) string
// fn lower(s string) string
// These functions change the case of a string.

// fn abs(x) / min(a, b) / max(a, b) / pow(x, y)
// These are simple numeric functions.

// fn rand() int
// fn srand(seed int)
// These functions implement a pseudo-random number generator.

// fn readFile(path string) string
// fn writeFile(path string, data string) bool
// fn appendFile(path string, data string) bool
// fn fileExists(path string) bool
// These functions provide basic file access.

// type any
// any is the empty interface. A value of any type (and nil) converts to any implicitly.
// An any holds a data pointer and a type descriptor, and is reference counted like an interface value.
// An interface value converts to any with its dynamic type. a == nil is true when the any holds nothing.

// Variadic functions
// A function or method may declare a variadic last parameter as name ...T. Inside the function it is a []T.
// A call packs the trailing arguments into a new array; f(xs...) passes an existing []T as it is.
// Variadic parameters are not allowed in extern functions or interface methods.

// fn typeKind(a any) int
// typeKind returns the kind of the dynamic type: 0 nil, 1 int, 2 int64, 3 float, 4 char, 5 byte, 6 bool,
// 7 string, 8 array, 9 pointer, 10 struct.

// fn typeName(a any) string
// typeName returns the name of the dynamic type, such as "int", "[]string", "*Point" or "geom.Circle"; "nil" for nil.

// fn anyInt(a any) int64
// anyInt returns the value of an int, int64, char or byte. Any other kind panics.

// fn anyFloat(a any) float
// fn anyBool(a any) bool
// fn anyString(a any) string
// These functions return the value of a float, bool or string. Any other kind panics.

// fn anyLen(a any) int
// anyLen returns the length of an array.

// fn anyIndex(a any, i int) any
// anyIndex returns the element i of an array as an any. An element of interface or any type is returned with its dynamic type.

// fn anyNumField(a any) int
// anyNumField returns the number of fields of a struct.

// fn anyFieldName(a any, i int) string
// anyFieldName returns the name of the field i of a struct.

// fn anyField(a any, i int) any
// anyField returns the field i of a struct as an any.

// fn anyElem(a any) any
// anyElem returns the value a pointer points to. For a nil pointer it returns nil.

// fn anyIsNil(a any) bool
// anyIsNil reports whether the any is nil or holds a nil pointer.

// fn anyAddr(a any) int64
// anyAddr returns the address stored in a pointer, or 0 for any other kind.

// fn anySlotIface(a any, i int) bool
// anySlotIface reports whether the element (arrays) or field i (structs) is declared with an interface or any type.

// fn anySetInt(p any, v int64)
// fn anySetFloat(p any, v float)
// fn anySetBool(p any, v bool)
// fn anySetString(p any, v string)
// fn anySetChar(p any, v char)
// These functions store a value through a pointer held in p. They panic if p is not a pointer to the matching type;
// anySetInt accepts a pointer to int, int64 or byte.
